Float and double overloads for erf and erfc

$(REF erf, std, mathspecial) and $(REF erfc, std, mathspecial) now return
`float` or `double` for arguments of those types, using the corresponding
Cephes single- and double-precision implementations.
Integral arguments continue to return `real`.

This changes the type inferred by `auto` and the precision and range of
intermediate results. In particular, tails representable in an extended
`real` may underflow when returned as `float` or `double`. Code that needs
the previous precision or range should explicitly cast the argument:
-------
auto extendedTail = erfc(cast(real) x);
-------
The existing `real` overloads are retained.

Floating-point enum arguments can become ambiguous between these overloads;
cast them explicitly to the desired floating-point type. Integer enums retain
the real-returning integral overloads.

The new `erfc` overloads inherit Cephes underflow cutoffs: some representable
subnormal results are returned as zero, including `erfc(10.0f)` and `erfc(27.0)`.
